{{also|re-lay}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Relay (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|frm|relai||reserve pack of hounds}}, from {{m|frm|relaier||to exchange tired animals for fresh}}; literally, "to leave behind", from {{der|en|fro|relaier||to leave behind}}, from {{m|fro|re-}} + {{m|fro|laier||to leave}}, of uncertain origin.

====Pronunciation====
* {{qualifier|noun}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːleɪ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-relay (noun).wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{qualifier|verb}} {{IPA|en|/ɹiˈleɪ/|/ˈɹiːleɪ/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-relay (verb).wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːleɪ|s=2}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|hunting|rare}} A new set of [[hound]]s. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|historical}} A new set of [[horse]]s kept along a specific [[route]] so that they can replace animals that are tired. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A new set of anything.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Dombey and Son|chapter=Retribution|page=594|passage=There is a snaky gleam in her hard grey eye, as of anticipated rounds of buttered toast, '''relays''' of hot chops, worryings and quellings of young children, sharp snappings at poor Berry, and all the other delights of her Ogress's castle.}}
# A [[series]] of [[vehicle]]s travelling in [[sequence]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|athletics}} A [[track and field]] discipline where runners take turns in carrying a [[baton]] from start to finish. Most common events are 4x100 meter and 4x400 meter competitions. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} An electrical [[actuator]] that allows a relatively small electrical [[voltage]] or [[current]] to control a larger voltage or current. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

